{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791608176,"nonce":"kQhH52jLX2DBLfjo6YuTUGfTIi8=","title":"Linking LLVM costs every process about 2.6 ms before the first instruction","message":"A light zylua (no llvm-backend, 25 MB) and the performance one (llvm-backend, 98.6 MB after the target trim) differ only in what the image carries, yet on the Mac (warm page cache, 20 interleaved rounds, medians):\n\n- `zylua backend` (exits before any runtime set-up): 4.66 ms light vs 7.26 ms LLVM; 21.1M vs 41.7M instructions; 333 vs 1089 page reclaims; 20k vs 88k dyld fixups; 0 vs 401 static initialisers.\n- empty program 8.87 vs 11.71 ms; fib to its first statement 10.45 vs 13.47; nbody 49.05 vs 52.32.\n- luajit on an empty file: 4.72 ms. The light build's exec cost equals LuaJIT's whole run.\n\nWith an injected constructor reading the process's retired instructions: before the image's initialisers run, 56.1M (LLVM) vs 42.7M (light); initialisers plus `main` plus exit 8.0M vs 1.0M. So about 13M instructions go to mapping the larger image and applying 68k more fixups, and 7M to LLVM's own initialisers.\n\nComparisons that run with the LLVM tier off pay all of it. Ways out, roughly in order of reach:\n- Load the LLVM tier on first use: the backend as a separate library dlopen'ed by the thread that first asks for an LLVM compile, so a run that never promotes to LLVM never maps it. The work is the interface: the backend reads compiler-wide state (runtime symbol tables, osr registries, the type interner), which a second copy of zyntax_compiler inside the library would not share, so everything it reads has to cross the boundary explicitly.\n- Defer LLVM's static initialisers (Mach-O: link with LLVM's __mod_init_func renamed and run them before the first LLVM call). Saves the 7M of initialisers only, and relies on every C++ initialiser in the image being LLVM's.\n- Compare with LLVM off using the light build.","files":null}]}